    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0b86fc72-df5b-54de-bbd6-4ef3615bd523",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "A production process uses $Q(x)$ squared meters of material to make $x$ packages. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have that the quantity $Q(x)$ is measured in squared meters and the input $x$ is measured in packages. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x): & \\text{squared meters} \\\\ x: & \\text{packages}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}$ measures a change in $Q(x)$ over a change in $x.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}: & \\frac{\\text{squared meters}}{\\text{packages}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}$ is measured in \"squared meters per package\".",
