      ":question/id": "b76a707c-559e-596a-94b2-80c91183a360",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "A farmer estimates that she can sell $w(p)$ metric kilograms of crops at a price of $p$ dollars per kilogram. What are the units of $\\frac{dw}{dp}$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have that $w(p)$ is measured in kilograms and $p$ is measured in dollars per kilogram. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w(p): & \\text{kilograms} \\\\ p: & \\text{dollars per kilogram}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}$ measures a change in $w(p)$ over a change in $p.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}: & \\frac{\\text{kilograms}}{\\text{dollars per kilogram}}=\\frac{\\text{squared kilograms}}{\\text{dollars}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}w}{\\text{d}p}$ is measured in \"squared kilograms per dollar\".",
