      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "A supplier sells $Q(x)$ meters of a product at a price of $x$ dollars per meter. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have that the quantity $Q(x)$ is measured in meters and the input $x$ is measured in dollars per meter. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x): & \\text{meters} \\\\ x: & \\text{dollars per meter}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}$ measures a change in $Q(x)$ over a change in $x.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}: & \\frac{\\text{meters}}{\\text{dollars per meter}}=\\frac{\\text{squared meters}}{\\text{dollars}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\text{d}Q}{\\text{d}x}$ is measured in \"squared meters per dollar\".",
